% array_bool_or, satisfiable, with a wrong guess so the reified decomposition's
% clauses are exercised in BOTH directions and one of them is the conflict.
%
% compile.ml decomposes `array_bool_or([p, q], r)', i.e. r <-> (p \/ q), into three
% clauses:
%
%   forward   (~r \/ p \/ q)      "if r then one of them"
%   backward  (r \/ ~p)           "if p then r"
%   backward  (r \/ ~q)           "if q then r"
%
% and this model makes the search use the forward clause as a CONFLICT and a backward
% clause as a UNIT PROPAGATION, which is the pair a one-directional decomposition would
% get wrong:
%
%   decide p = false  (indomain_min)
%     -> `p \/ r' makes r true
%     -> `p \/ ~q' makes q false
%     -> forward clause (~r \/ p \/ q) is violated: r true, p false, q false.  CONFLICT
%   so p = true
%     -> backward clause (r \/ ~p) makes r true
%     -> q is free, indomain_min settles it false
%
% Ground truth: `p \/ r' and `p \/ ~q' and r <-> (p \/ q). If p were false then r is
% true and q is false, so p \/ q is false while r is true -- contradiction. So p is
% true, hence r is true; q = false then satisfies everything. p = true and r = true are
% both the opposite of the indomain_min default, so an ignored or one-way
% `array_bool_or' cannot print this answer.
var bool: p :: output_var;
var bool: q :: output_var;
var bool: r :: output_var;
constraint array_bool_or([p, q], r);
constraint bool_clause([p, r], []);
constraint bool_clause([p], [q]);
solve satisfy;
